<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qifei Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhen Gao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhijin Qin
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiaodong Xu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Meixia Tao
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Qifei Wang is with the School of Information and Electronics, Beijing Institute of Technology, Beijing 100081, China (e-mail: <a href="mailto:qfwang@bit.edu.cn" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">qfwang@bit.edu.cn</a>).</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Zhen Gao (<span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">corresponding author</span>) is with State Key Laboratory of CNS/ATM, Beijing Institute of Technology (BIT), Beijing 100081, China, also with BIT Zhuhai 519088, China, also with the MIT Key Laboratory of Complex-Field Intelligent Sensing, BIT, Beijing 100081, China, also with the Advanced Technology Research Institute of BIT (Jinan), Jinan 250307, China, and also with the Yangtze Delta Region Academy, BIT (Jiaxing), Jiaxing 314019, China (e-mail: <a href="mailto:gaozhen16@bit.edu.cn" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">gaozhen16@bit.edu.cn</a>).</span></span></span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Zhijin Qin is with the Department of Electronic Engineering, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China. She is also with the State Key Laboratory of Space Network and Communications and the Beijing National Research Center for Information Science and Technology, Beijing 100084, China (e-mail: <a href="mailto:qinzhijin@tsinghua.edu.cn" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">qinzhijin@tsinghua.edu.cn</a>).</span></span></span><span id="id4" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Xiaodong Xu is with the State Key Laboratory of Networking and Switching Technology, Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications, Beijing 100876, China, and also with Peng Cheng Laboratory, Shenzhen 518066, China (e-mail: <a href="mailto:xuxiaodong@bupt.edu.cn" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">xuxiaodong@bupt.edu.cn</a>).</span></span></span><span id="id5" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Meixia Tao is with the Cooperative Medianet Innovation Center and the Department of Electronic Engineering, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai 200240, China (e-mail: <a href="mailto:mxtao@sjtu.edu.cn" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">mxtao@sjtu.edu.cn</a>).</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">As a promising 6G enabler beyond conventional bit-level transmission, semantic communication can considerably reduce required bandwidth resources, while its combination with multiple access requires further exploration. This paper proposes a knowledge distillation-driven and diffusion-enhanced (KDD) semantic non-orthogonal multiple access (NOMA), named KDD-SemNOMA, for multi-user uplink wireless image transmission. Specifically, to ensure robust feature transmission across diverse transmission conditions, we firstly develop a ConvNeXt-based deep joint source and channel coding architecture with enhanced adaptive feature module. This module incorporates signal-to-noise ratio and channel state information to dynamically adapt to additive white Gaussian noise and Rayleigh fading channels. Furthermore, to improve image restoration quality without inference overhead, we introduce a two-stage knowledge distillation strategy, i.e., a teacher model, trained on interference-free orthogonal transmission, guides a student model via feature affinity distillation and cross-head prediction distillation. Moreover, a diffusion model-based refinement stage leverages generative priors to transform initial SemNOMA outputs into high-fidelity images with enhanced perceptual quality. Extensive experiments on CIFAR-10 and FFHQ-256 datasets demonstrate superior performance over state-of-the-art methods, delivering satisfactory reconstruction performance even at extremely poor channel conditions. These results highlight the advantages in both pixel-level accuracy and perceptual metrics, effectively mitigating interference and enabling high-quality image recovery.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Semantic communication, non-orthogonal multiple access, channel adaptation, knowledge distillation, diffusion model.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the gradual advancement of 6G wireless networks, wireless communication is entering a transformative era of technological innovation. Future 6G networks will not only accommodate an explosive growth in data traffic but also meet escalating user demands for intelligent and efficient connection services <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In this context, traditional bit-oriented communication paradigm has gradually revealed limitations. Among the promising candidates for 6G, semantic communication has garnered significant attention due to its potential to transcend the constraints of conventional bit-level transmission <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. By integrating artificial intelligence, semantic communication shifts transmission objectives from bit-level to semantic-level information delivery, demonstrating superior transmission efficiency over traditional separated source-channel coding methods in bandwidth-constrained and complex channel environments. However, designing end-to-end semantic communication systems for multi-user scenarios face significant challenges: adapting to diverse channel conditions (e.g., additive white Gaussian noise [AWGN] channel and Rayleigh fading channel), mitigating non-orthogonal feature interference, and enhancing perceptual quality under strict bandwidth constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Specifically, non-orthogonal transmission, where multiple users’ semantic features share the same time-frequency resources, significantly exacerbates interference, while diverse channels and low compression ratios degrade signal reconstruction quality. This paper presents a novel solution to address these challenges, thereby advancing the practical deployment of semantic communication in 6G networks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span> </span><span id="S1.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Related Works</span></h3>

<section id="S1.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span>1 </span>DeepJSCC in Single-User and Multi-User Semantic Communication</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic communication has achieved remarkable progress in single-user scenarios, with numerous studies employing deep joint source-channel coding (DeepJSCC) to enable efficient semantic transmission of diverse modalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. The paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> introduces attention modules to adaptive signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) in DeepJSCC codec, and the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> introduces learnable entropy models to implement joint source-channel coding (JSCC) with variable-length transmission mechanism. These works underscore the potential of semantic communication in terms of dynamic performance and robustness. However, research on multi-user semantic communication remains in its infancy, with design complexity far exceeding that of single-user systems. In multi-user settings, semantic interference, limited transmission resources, and inter-user channel contention emerge as primary bottlenecks. The authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> propose task-oriented frameworks for multi-user semantic communication, while the authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> explores the feasibility of multimodal semantic fusion. The paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> combines beamforming algorithm and semantic coding in the downlink massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) scenario to achieve multi-user image downlink semantic transmission. Nonetheless, these approaches predominantly rely on orthogonal resource allocation, resulting in a sharp increase in physical-layer overhead as the number of users grows. To address this, non-orthogonal multiple access (NOMA) concepts have been introduced. For instance, the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> proposes the end-to-end (E2E) uplink DeepJSCC-NOMA network for wireless image transmission; the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> leverages NOMA with quantization modules to enable intelligent multi-user detection; the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> employs semantic differential superposition coding at the transmitter and interference cancellation at the receiver to support both homogeneous and heterogeneous transmission (i.e., semantic information and bit streams); the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> extracts and decorrelates basic and enhanced source features, then superposes them probabilistically for transmission, allowing receivers to decode semantic information commensurate with channel quality. However, these methods are still insufficient in terms of interference optimization in NOMA scenarios and cannot be applied to dynamic channel conditions. Additionally, the authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> proposes Swin-Transformer-based feature fusion schemes under degraded broadcast channels (DBC) to reduce transmission overhead via joint representations, though the high computational complexity limits scalability in multi-user scenarios. Novel semantic multiple-access schemes have also emerged: the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> exploits DeepJSCC’s interference resilience to propose an orthogonal model division approach, while the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> introduces semantic feature multiple access for multi-user video transmission. Despite integrating traditional multiple-access principles with neural network robustness, these solutions lack systematic strategies for interference elimination and resource optimization in multi-user non-orthogonal settings. This paper focuses on uplink multi-user non-orthogonal semantic transmission and designs a low-complexity deep learning framework that can adapt to different channel conditions and further optimize to reduce the impact of interference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span>2 </span>Knowledge Distillation and Its Application in Semantic Communication</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Knowledge distillation, first proposed by Hinton et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, aims to transfer the representational capacity of teacher model to student model. The authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> categorize distilled knowledge into three types: target knowledge, encompassing soft targets from the teacher model typically used in classification tasks; feature knowledge, derived from intermediate layer outputs; and relationship knowledge, capturing inter-layer or inter-sample relationships.
This framework has been widely used in tasks such as object detection and image super-resolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Recently, knowledge distillation has been adapted to semantic communication. For example, the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> enhances the performance of users with varying computational capabilities in downlink multi-user scenarios, outperforming iterative training methods, while the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> leverages distillation to address multi-user interference and random noise, improving classification performance in text-based semantic systems. However, these studies primarily focus on optimizing performance for specific desired models, neglecting the overall system’s average performance, and have not yet to explore the application of knowledge distillation in multi-user non-orthogonal transmission scenario. Moreover, their reliance on prediction mimicry for model compression risks misalignment between the student network’s optimization objectives and semantic transmission requirements. This paper introduces knowledge distillation into uplink multi-user image semantic NOMA transmission, which achieves a comprehensive improvement in the quality of image pixel reconstruction without increasing the inference overhead.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span>3 </span>Diffusion Model and Its Application in Semantic Communication</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conventional DeepJSCC frameworks typically aim to minimize pixel-level distortion, such as mean squared error (MSE), as their optimization objective, but they ignore the improvement of image perceptual quality, especially in poor channel environments. In recent years, generative models have achieved groundbreaking progress in computer vision, with diffusion models emerging as a superior alternative to GANs due to their capacity to produce high-quality and semantically coherent images. Within the domain of semantic communication, GANs have been employed to improve image transmission quality. For instance, the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> models the entire DeepJSCC transmission pipeline as the forward process, leveraging a pretrained StyleGAN2 to solve the inverse problem and improve perceptual quality. In contrast, diffusion models offer distinct advantages in stability, resistance to mode collapse, and generative diversity. Accordingly, the authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> introduce the diffusion-aided JSCC framework, utilizing a pretrained Stable Diffusion model to the image reconstruction with initial JSCC decoding and multimodal conditions (spatial, textual, and channel state information). Similarly, the works of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> deploy pretrained diffusion models at the receiver to boost perceptual quality, Furthermore, the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> proposes channel denoising diffusion models (CDDM), which leverage diffusion models to preprocess channel outputs. The paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> presents the semantics-guided diffusion DeepJSCC (SGD-JSCC) scheme, which integrates diffusion models with multi-modal semantic guidance (i.e., text and edge maps) to design a channel denoising model. Inspired by these advancements, this paper innovatively proposes a two-stage image reconstruction framework tailored for semantic NOMA scenarios, deploying pretrained diffusion models at the receiver to achieve superior perceptual quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">I-B</span> </span><span id="S1.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Motivation and Contributions</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although prior studies have leveraged deep learning techniques to design multi-user semantic communication systems, significant challenges persist in the context of uplink semantic NOMA transmission scenarios. These include adapting to dynamic channel conditions such as AWGN and Rayleigh fading channels, inadequate management of non-orthogonal semantic feature interference, and limited enhancements in multi-user perceptual image quality under strict bandwidth constraints. Dynamic channels degrade transmission performance, interference from superimposed different users’ signals reduces pixel-level fidelity, and high compression ratios and poor channel conditions impair perceptual metrics. These limitations hinder scalable 6G network deployment. Addressing these challenges in semantic NOMA communication, this paper proposes a novel framework that enhances image recovery performance across both pixel-level fidelity and perceptual quality. The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-orthogonal semantic transmission framework</span>: We propose a semantic NOMA framework (SemNOMA) for multi-user image uplink transmission, where a ConvNeXt-based DeepJSCC network is designed and an enhanced channel feature adaptation module is incorporated to ensure robust performance across AWGN and Rayleigh fading channels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Knowledge distillation for non-orthogonal transmission optimization</span>: We innovatively incorporate knowledge distillation scheme into the SemNOMA framework, called KD-SemNOMA, where an orthogonal transmission-based teacher model is utilized to guide SemNOMA training via feature affinity and cross-head distillation. Simulations demonstrate that the introduction of the knowledge distillation framework yields a peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) performance gain of approximately <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2\sim 0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2\sim 0.8</annotation></semantics></math>dB compared with DeepJSCC-NOMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, significantly enhancing the SemNOMA restoration performance without incurring additional computational overhead during inference.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion model-enhanced image refinement</span>: We further propose a two-stage reconstruction approach, called KDD-SemNOMA, where initial KD-SemNOMA (the first stage) outputs are refined by exploiting the error contraction property and generative priors of pretrained diffusion models (the second stage), leading to substantial improvements in perceptual quality and detail fidelity. Extensive experiments on different datasets have shown that our method significantly outperforms previous multi-user DeepJSCC NOMA methods in terms of image pixel distortion and perceptual metrics, demonstrating its capability to preserve the original transmitted image semantics.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of this paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, we introduce the system model and describe the overall structure of SemNOMA framework. In Section <a href="#S3" title="III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, we introduce the knowledge distillation strategy to optimize SemNOMA. In Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, we show that the diffusion prior can further recover the details of the reconstructed images. Numerical simulation results are provided in Section <a href="#S5" title="V Training and Evaluation ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>, followed by the conclusion of this work in Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Conclusion ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notation</span>: <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the real and complex number sets, respectively. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{CN}\left(\mu,\sigma^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{CN}\left(\mu,\sigma^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Gaussian function and the complex form of the Gaussian function with mean <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\cdot\right\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\cdot\right\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm (sum of absolute values). <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes indexing into a multi-dimensional array or tensor. Superscript <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)^{\mathrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)^{\mathrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transpose. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times m</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{A}|_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{A}|_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of elements in the matrix <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">System Model</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overview</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed KDD-SemNOMA framework is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ II-A Overview ‣ II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Consider an uplink multi-user image semantic communication scenario where multiple user equipment (UE) transmit uplink image semantic information to the base station (BS). Unlike conventional communication systems employing separate source and channel coding, our framework adopts JSCC to directly transmit semantic features. Both UE and BS are configured with single antennas. Each UE transmits an image <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the index of UE, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> represent the color channels, width, and height of the RGB image, respectively. We denote <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=C\times H\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=C\times H\times W</annotation></semantics></math>. The transmitter encodes the source image <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into compressed semantic features <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{i}\in\mathbb{C}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{i}\in\mathbb{C}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> through a JSCC encoder, which can be expressed as</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07363v1/Figure0.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:349/132;" width="349" height="132" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the proposed KDD-SemNOMA Framework.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{s}_{i}}=\mathcal{E}_{i}({\mathbf{x}_{i};\mathbf{\Theta}_{i}}),i\in\{1,2,\cdots,N\},\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{s}_{i}}=\mathcal{E}_{i}({\mathbf{x}_{i};\mathbf{\Theta}_{i}}),i\in\{1,2,\cdots,N\},\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of UE, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{i}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{i}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the encoder neural network of the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE with the trainable parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Theta}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Theta}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The bandwidth compression ratio <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the available channel symbols, is defined as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{k}{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{k}{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
An average transmit power constraint is imposed on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{k}\left\|\mathbf{s}_{i}\right\|_{2}^{2}\leq P_{\text{avg}}.\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{k}\left\|\mathbf{s}_{i}\right\|_{2}^{2}\leq P_{\text{avg}}.\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To reduce transmission overhead, the semantic features from multiple users are non-orthogonally superimposed on the same time-frequency resources and transmitted through the channel. The received signal at the BS can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}{h}_{i}\mathbf{s}_{i}+\mathbf{n},\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}{h}_{i}\mathbf{s}_{i}+\mathbf{n},\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the wireless channel gain between the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE and the BS, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{C}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{C}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) complex Gaussian noise term with variance <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. For AWGN channel, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. For the Rayleigh fading channel, the channel gain <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE follows <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_{h}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_{h}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{h}^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{h}^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the normalized channel power. We assume <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are known at both transmitter and receiver. The SNR of the wireless channel is defined as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=10\log_{10}\left(\frac{P_{\text{avg}}}{\sigma^{2}}\right)\mathrm{dB}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dB</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=10\log_{10}\left(\frac{P_{\text{avg}}}{\sigma^{2}}\right)\mathrm{dB}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At the receiver, the JSCC decoder reconstructs the multi-user image based on the superimposed semantic features, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}=\mathcal{D}_{i}(\mathbf{y};\mathbf{\Psi}_{i}),i\in\{1,2,\cdots,N\},\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}=\mathcal{D}_{i}(\mathbf{y};\mathbf{\Psi}_{i}),i\in\{1,2,\cdots,N\},\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{i}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{i}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the decoder neural network for the <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE with the trainable parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Psi}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Psi}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reconstructed image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework for multi-user image reconstruction at the receiver comprises two primary stages: (1) multi-user DeepJSCC decoding optimized by knowledge distillation as detailed in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, and (2) image refinement guided by diffusion-based generative priors as detailed in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. At the first stage, the SemNOMA model is trained using knowledge distillation to effectively decode transmitted signals. At the second stage, we leverage pretrained diffusion model priors to refine low-quality received images, where their visual fidelity and detail have further enhanced.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Enhanced AF-Module</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, to improve the adaptability and robustness of the SemNOMA framework under varying channel conditions, we propose an enhanced attention feature module (AF-Module), as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Enhanced AF-Module ‣ II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The module dynamically modulates image semantic features using channel side information, including SNR and Rayleigh fading parameters, enabling robust performance across diverse channel environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let the input semantic feature map be <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}\times W^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}\times W^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote batch size, number of channels, height, and width, respectively. The channel side information is represented by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the SNR <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,20]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,20]</annotation></semantics></math> dB and Rayleigh fading parameters of the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE (amplitude <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and phase <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). During training, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly sampled from <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,20]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,20]</annotation></semantics></math> dB, and the Rayleigh parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i},\phi_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i},\phi_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly generated to simulate diverse channel conditions. This enhances the model’s adaptability and reduces the impact of channel on the semantic features.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07363v1/Figure3.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:359/112;" width="359" height="112"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Detail architecture of the enhanced AF-Module.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The enhanced AF-Module processes <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> through the following steps:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spatial context extraction</span>: The spatial context feature <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{\text{ctx}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mtext>ctx</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{\text{ctx}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by averaging <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> over spatial dimensions</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{\text{ctx}}=\frac{1}{H^{\prime}\cdot W^{\prime}}\sum_{h=1}^{H^{\prime}}\sum_{w=1}^{W^{\prime}}\mathbf{f}_{s}[b,c,h,w].\vskip-5.69054pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mtext>ctx</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{\text{ctx}}=\frac{1}{H^{\prime}\cdot W^{\prime}}\sum_{h=1}^{H^{\prime}}\sum_{w=1}^{W^{\prime}}\mathbf{f}_{s}[b,c,h,w].\vskip-5.69054pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Channel feature encoding</span>: The side information <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is encoded into a channel feature <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> via a channel encoder <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{c}=\mathcal{F}_{c}(\mathbf{s}),\vskip-5.69054pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{c}=\mathcal{F}_{c}(\mathbf{s}),\vskip-5.69054pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of two linear layers with LayerNorm and Leaky rectified linear unit (LeakyReLU) activation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature fusion</span>: The spatial context and channel features are concatenated to form <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 2C^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 2C^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{f}=\operatorname{Concat}(\mathbf{f}_{\text{ctx}},\mathbf{f}_{c}),\vskip-5.69054pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Concat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mtext>ctx</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{f}=\operatorname{Concat}(\mathbf{f}_{\text{ctx}},\mathbf{f}_{c}),\vskip-5.69054pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Concat}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Concat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Concat}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> operates along the channel dimension.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive mask generation</span>: An adaptive mask <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times 1\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times 1\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated via a fusion network <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}=\mathcal{F}_{m}(\mathbf{f}_{f}),\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}=\mathcal{F}_{m}(\mathbf{f}_{f}),\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises three linear layers with LeakyReLU, <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> dropout, and sigmoid function.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature modulation</span>: The semantic features <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are modulated using the mask:</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{\text{out}}=\mathbf{f}_{s}\odot\operatorname{Broadcast}(\mathbf{m}),\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mi>Broadcast</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{\text{out}}=\mathbf{f}_{s}\odot\operatorname{Broadcast}(\mathbf{m}),\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Broadcast}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Broadcast</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Broadcast}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> aligns <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times 1\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times 1\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the spatial dimensions of <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}\times W^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}\times W^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes element-wise multiplication.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SemNOMA Network Architecture</span></h3>

<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07363v1/Figure1.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:419/143;" width="419" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Architecture of the ConvNeXt-based DeepJSCC Network, where Conv2D <math id="S2.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow 2</annotation></semantics></math> represents 2-dimensional convolution with stride=2 for downsampling.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-C SemNOMA Network Architecture ‣ II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates the proposed DeepJSCC neural network, designed for efficient compression and reconstruction of image source over noisy channels. The architecture is based on the modified ConvNeXt framework<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, where downsampling, upsampling, and attention modules are incorporated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The ConvNeXt block, which modernizes ResNet with Vision Transformer-inspired designs, achieving state-of-the-art on ImageNet classification task and outperforming Swin Transformer on COCO detection challenges while maintaining the maturity and simplicity of convolutional neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-C SemNOMA Network Architecture ‣ II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, each ConvNeXt block processes input features of dimension <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> through a sequence of operations: a depthwise convolution with a <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\times 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times 7</annotation></semantics></math> kernel, followed by a LayerNorm applied in a channels-last format. This is succeeded by a pointwise convolution implemented as a linear layer expanding the dimension to <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, a Gaussian error linear unit (GELU) activation for nonlinearity, and another linear layer reducing it back to <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. A residual connection with stochastic depth enhances training stability, balancing efficiency and expressive power for feature extraction in DeepJSCC.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The encoder processes RGB images and normalizes them to the <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> range by dividing pixel values by 255. It employs a stem layer for initial downsampling, followed by LayerNorm and additional downsampling stages. Multiple ConvNeXt blocks, with <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> blocks per stage, process features, as detailed in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A2 Training and Inference Details ‣ V-A Simulation Setup ‣ V Training and Evaluation ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> for 32<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>32 resolution and Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-A2 Training and Inference Details ‣ V-A Simulation Setup ‣ V Training and Evaluation ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> for 256<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>256 resolution. Enhanced AF-Module, as introduced in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="II-B Enhanced AF-Module ‣ II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-B</span></a>, improve the robustness to channel noise by conditioning on SNR and Rayleigh fading parameters (i.e., <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma,a,\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma,a,\phi</annotation></semantics></math>). The final <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> convolution produces <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> channels, normalized to meet the power constraint (i.e., Eq. <a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Overview ‣ II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), and reshaped into <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> complex-valued channel inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The decoder reconstructs image from the received multi-user superimposed semantic features. The <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> complex-valued samples are converted to <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> real-valued channels, processed by a <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> convolution and LayerNorm. Progressive upsampling stages then restore spatial resolution (e.g., <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math>), each followed by <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> ConvNeXt blocks with parametric rectified linear unit (PReLU) activations and AF-Modules for feature refinement, as specified in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A2 Training and Inference Details ‣ V-A Simulation Setup ‣ V Training and Evaluation ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> and Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-A2 Training and Inference Details ‣ V-A Simulation Setup ‣ V Training and Evaluation ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>. A final <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> convolution (<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=3</annotation></semantics></math>) with sigmoid activation produces a <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=3</annotation></semantics></math> output, which is then denormalized to range <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,255]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,255</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,255]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon the designed DeepJSCC architecture, we constructed a network for multi-user semantic non-orthogonal transmission scenarios, called SemNOMA. Specifically, at the transmitter, we employ a parameter-shared encoder to reduce computational overhead. Following the approach proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, we embed user-specific embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, initialized from <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, to enable the model to distinguish users. For each UE <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the input image <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> is concatenated with <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> along the channel dimension, forming the encoder input <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{(C+1)\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{(C+1)\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, expressed as</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}=\operatorname{Concat}(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{r}_{i}),\vskip-5.69054pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Concat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}=\operatorname{Concat}(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{r}_{i}),\vskip-5.69054pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Concat}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Concat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Concat}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes channel-wise concatenation, preserving spatial dimensions <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\times W</annotation></semantics></math>. At the receiver, multiple decoders are utilized to reconstruct user-specific images, enabling tailored reconstruction for each UE.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA)</span></h2>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Training and inference procedure for SemNOMA with knowledge distillation</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          Multi-user image dataset <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=\{\mathbf{x}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=\{\mathbf{x}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, hyperparameters <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          Reconstructed multi-user images <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}=\{\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{1},\dots,\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}=\{\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{1},\dots,\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Phase (Teacher &amp; Student)</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 1: Pre-train Teacher Model</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
          Initialize teacher model <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{teacher}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>teacher</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{teacher}}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
          Transmit features orthogonally according to Eq. <a href="#S3.E11" title="In III-A Teacher Model Training ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
          Optimize teacher model via MAE loss (Eq.<a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-A Teacher Model Training ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>):

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
          Freeze <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{teacher}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>teacher</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{teacher}}</annotation></semantics></math> weights after convergence

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 2: Train Student Model</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
          Initialize student model <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>student</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}</annotation></semantics></math> with teacher model weights

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each batch <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{batch}}\subseteq\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>batch</mtext></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{batch}}\subseteq\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
            Extract teacher model features <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and student model features <math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
            Compute affinity matrices <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}^{t},\mathbf{A}_{i}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}^{t},\mathbf{A}_{i}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq.<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-B Student Model Training ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
            
            
            Calculate feature affinity loss <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq.<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-B Student Model Training ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
            
            
            Generate cross-head predictions <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
            
            
            Compute CrossKD loss <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CrossKD</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq.<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-B Student Model Training ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
            
            
            Calculate restoration loss <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MAE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq.<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-B Student Model Training ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
            
            
            Total loss: <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{S}=\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MAE</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FA</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CrossKD</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{S}=\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
            
            
            Update <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>student</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="alg1.l19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathcal{L}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathcal{L}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l20.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l21.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference Phase (Student Only)</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l22" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l22.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">22:</span></span>
            
            
          Input transmisstion images <math id="alg1.l22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l23" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l23.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">23:</span></span>
            
            
          Forward pass through <math id="alg1.l23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>student</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}</annotation></semantics></math>:

</div>
<div id="alg1.l24" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l24.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">24:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{test}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>student</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{test}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l25" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l25.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">25:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The pipeline of our proposed knowledge distillation optimization strategy for SemNOMA (KD-SemNOMA) is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-A Teacher Model Training ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Multi-user images are processed through dual branches: the teacher model and the student model, both sharing identical network architectures. Orthogonal transmission of multi-user image semantic features is more conducive to training compared to non-orthogonal transmission, as orthogonal features are solely affected by the wireless channel noise, without interference from other users. Inspired by knowledge distillation scheme<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, we designate the E2E network with orthogonal transmission as the teacher model and the SemNOMA model as the student model. By effectively transferring user-specific semantic orthogonal knowledge from the teacher model to the student model, the decoding performance of the student SemNOMA model is further enhanced. The training process comprises two stages: pre-training the teacher model, followed by training the student model while freezing the weight parameters of the teacher model. During the inference phase, the teacher model branch is discarded, and only the student network is deployed, ensuring that our approach incurs no additional computational overhead for inference. The complete training and inference workflow of the knowledge distillation scheme is presented in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Teacher Model Training</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain interference-free feature vectors as “knowledge” for guiding the non-orthogonal transmission network training, we employ an orthogonal transmission scheme as the teacher model. In this configuration, multi-user image semantic features are transmitted orthogonally through the channel. For the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE, semantic features <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are transmitted through the channel as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{i}=h_{i}\mathbf{s}_{i}+\mathbf{n},\vskip-5.69054pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{i}=h_{i}\mathbf{s}_{i}+\mathbf{n},\vskip-5.69054pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">At the receiver, the decoder reconstructs the images with higher quality due to the absence of inter-user interference. We adopt the mean absolute error (MAE) as the loss function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to optimize the teacher model since its efficiency has been widely recognized in such tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\mathcal{F}_{\text{teacher}}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-\mathbf{x}_{i}\right\|_{1},\vskip-5.69054pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>teacher</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\mathcal{F}_{\text{teacher}}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-\mathbf{x}_{i}\right\|_{1},\vskip-5.69054pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{teacher}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>teacher</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{teacher}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the teacher network’s mapping function, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input image for the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07363v1/Figure2.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/190;" width="429" height="190" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The overall framework of KD-SemNOMA. The teacher model employs orthogonal channel for image semantic features transmission, while the student model utilizes non-orthogonal channel. <math id="S3.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.F4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer of the decoder. <math id="S3.F4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the student model output of the <math id="S3.F4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE, <math id="S3.F4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{i,0}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{i,0}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the input feature of the teacher decoder, <math id="S3.F4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAEi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MAEi</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAEi}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the MAE loss of the <math id="S3.F4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE, <math id="S3.F4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKDi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CrossKDi</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKDi}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the CrossKD loss of the <math id="S3.F4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE, <math id="S3.F4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{FAi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>FAi</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{FAi}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the FA loss of the <math id="S3.F4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Student Model Training</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the training phase of the student SemNOMA model, we initialize the weights of the student model with the parameters from the pre-trained teacher model. This transfers the reconstruction capability of the teacher model to the student model and provides a good starting point for optimization. To effectively transfer knowledge from the teacher model to the student model, we employ both feature distillation and cross head prediction distillation to enhance the learning capacity of the student model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For feature distillation, the objective is to align the latent semantic representations of the student and teacher models as closely as possible, thereby enabling the student model to reconstruct interference-free multi-user images. We select feature affinity (FA) knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> as the feature distillation loss function to extract high-dimensional feature semantic information. Specifically, for the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE, given a batch of image feature maps <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}\times W^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}\times W^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first reshape it into the three-dimensional tensor <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{i}^{{}^{\prime}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}W^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{i}^{{}^{\prime}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}W^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and normalize the feature map to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{f}}_{i}[b,:,w]=\mathbf{f}_{i}^{\prime}[b,:,w]/\left\|\mathbf{f}_{i}^{\prime}[b,:,w]\right\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{f}}_{i}[b,:,w]=\mathbf{f}_{i}^{\prime}[b,:,w]/\left\|\mathbf{f}_{i}^{\prime}[b,:,w]\right\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then compute the spatial affinity similarity matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times H^{\prime}W^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}W^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times H^{\prime}W^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}W^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> by inner product, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}=\tilde{\mathbf{f}}_{i}^{T}\cdot\tilde{\mathbf{f}}_{i}.\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}=\tilde{\mathbf{f}}_{i}^{T}\cdot\tilde{\mathbf{f}}_{i}.\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The student model is encouraged to produce similar affinity matrices with teacher model and the feature affinity-based distillation loss <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be formulated as</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{|\mathbf{A}_{i}|_{c}}\sum_{j=1}^{L}\left\|\mathbf{A}_{i,j}^{s}-\mathbf{A}_{i,j}^{t}\right\|_{1}.\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FA</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{|\mathbf{A}_{i}|_{c}}\sum_{j=1}^{L}\left\|\mathbf{A}_{i,j}^{s}-\mathbf{A}_{i,j}^{t}\right\|_{1}.\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Note that we utilize the MAE loss to optimize the similarity of the spatial feature affinity matrices, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i,j}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i,j}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i,j}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i,j}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the affinity matrices for the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE, extracted from the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer feature maps of the teacher and student models respectively. <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of layers we choose to extract features. <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{A}_{i}|_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{A}_{i}|_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of elements in the affinity matrix. In particular, we adopt the input features of the teacher and student models’ decoders to calculate the feature distillation loss, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For prediction distillation, instead of directly minimizing the discrepancy between the predictions of teacher model and the student model, we adopt the cross-head knowledge distillation (CrossKD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> to alleviate the conflict between the supervised and distillation targets. Specifically, CrossKD transfers the intermediate semantic features from the student model’s head to that of the teacher model to produce the cross head predictions for distillation, which implicitly builds the connection between the heads of the teacher-student pair to improve the distillation efficiency. In particular, we implement CrossKD on the decoder. For the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE, let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{i,j},j\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{i,j},j\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math> denoted the feature maps produced by the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th decoder layer <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{i,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{i,0}</annotation></semantics></math> being the input feature map of the decoder. The restored images of the teacher and the student model of the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE can be represented as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Besides the original restoration from the teacher and the student, CrossKD additionally delivers the student model’s intermediate features <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{i,j},j\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{i,j},j\in\{1,2,\cdots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th decoder layer <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the teacher model, resulting in the cross-head restoration <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}^{s}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}^{s}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similar to the feature distillation loss function, we use MAE loss to optimize the image restoration result. The CrossKD objective <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CrossKD</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}}</annotation></semantics></math> is described as follows</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{s}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{t}\right\|_{1}.\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CrossKD</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{s}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{t}\right\|_{1}.\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the MAE loss <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MAE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}</annotation></semantics></math> between the ground truth and the output of the student model can be denoted as</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-\mathbf{x}_{i}\right\|_{1},\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MAE</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>student</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-\mathbf{x}_{i}\right\|_{1},\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>student</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{student}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the student network’s mapping function. The total loss <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for training the student model can be formulated as the weighted sum of the image restoration loss and the distillation loss, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}},\vskip-4.2679pt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MAE</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FA</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CrossKD</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FA}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{CrossKD}},\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> govern the trade-off among different aspects of the loss.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement</span></h2>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07363v1/Figure4.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:349/147;" width="349" height="147"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Block diagram of the image refinement based on the pre-trained diffusion model (KDD-SemNOMA).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the first stage, the proposed KD-SemNOMA framework, trained via knowledge distillation to leverage the semantic knowledge of the teacher model, recovers multi-user images optimized for pixel-level accuracy using MAE. However, this approach often yields suboptimal perceptual quality, particularly under poor channel conditions (e.g., low SNR). To address this, inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, we further propose a second-stage diffusion model based image restoration method, called KDD-SemNOMA. This leverages the error contraction property of diffusion process and the image priors encapsulated in the pre-trained diffusion model to enhance semantic and perceptual quality of the reconstruction images.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Preliminary</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion model comprises a forward (diffusion) process and a reverse process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Given a data sample <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with the probability distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward process progressively adds Gaussian noise through <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps according to a pre-defined or learned noise variance schedule <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. This process is defined as a Markov chain with the conditional probability distribution, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{x}_{{t}-{1}}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{t-1},\beta_{t}\mathbf{I}_{m}\right),\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{x}_{{t}-{1}}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{t-1},\beta_{t}\mathbf{I}_{m}\right),\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\{1,2,\cdots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\{1,2,\cdots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A well-designed variance schedule theoretically guarantees that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\left(\mathbf{x}_{T}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\mathbf{x}_{T}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> converges to the simple Gaussian distribution. Remarkably, the marginal distribution at arbitrary timestep <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> has the following analytical form, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{0},(1-\alpha_{t})\mathbf{I}_{m}),\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{0},(1-\alpha_{t})\mathbf{I}_{m}),\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=\prod_{l=1}^{t}\left(1-\beta_{l}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=\prod_{l=1}^{t}\left(1-\beta_{l}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The reverse process learns a transition kernel from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> which is defined as the following Gaussian distribution, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1};\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}_{m}),\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1};\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}_{m}),\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes learnable parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The denoising diffusion implicit model (DDIM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> inference framework generalizes the denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> to an non-Markov process, the reverse posterior is expressed as</p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E21X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}^{(t)}+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}^{(t)}+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E21Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\eta\cdot\frac{1-\alpha_{t-1}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\beta_{t},\quad\eta\in[0,1]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\eta\cdot\frac{1-\alpha_{t-1}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\beta_{t},\quad\eta\in[0,1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}^{(t)}=\frac{\mathbf{x}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t\right)}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}.\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0}^{(t)}=\frac{\mathbf{x}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t\right)}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}.\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">The hyper-parameter <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> controls the extent of randomness during inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The marginal distribution of Eq. <a href="#S4.E19" title="In IV-A Preliminary ‣ IV Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> reveals that the initial state <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is contracted by a scaling factor <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> after transitioning to timestep <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. As demonstrated by recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, when <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is interpreted as a coarse estimated image, this marginal distribution inherently compresses the prediction error associated with <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Motivated by this insight, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we propose to treat the image estimated by KD-SemNOMA as the initial estimate <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By leveraging the error contraction property of diffusion process, we first apply forward diffusion to <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then employ the reverse diffusion process to iteratively denoise <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby enhancing the image perceptual quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion-Based Image Refinement</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our proposed KDD-SemNOMA scheme, we first leverage the proposed KD-SemNOMA model (as depicted in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>) to reconstruct images <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}=\hat{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}=\hat{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> from multi-user non-orthogonal transmissions (Eq. <a href="#S2.E4" title="In II-A Overview ‣ II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). We denote the real high-quality images as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the first-stage reconstructed images from the KD-SemNOMA model as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to compression losses, channel noise and multi-user interference inherent in the semantic NOMA scenario, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math> deviates from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, introducing reconstruction errors. To refine <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math> into a high-fidelity approximation of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we propose designing a posterior distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}})</annotation></semantics></math>, inspired by the diffusion-based image enhancement strategy in DifFace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The refinement process consists of two primary stages, as depicted below:</p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{\mathbf{x}\xrightarrow[\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}=\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x};\mathbf{\Omega})]{\text{KD-SemNOMA}}\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}\xrightarrow[\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}\sim p(\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}\mid\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}})]{\text{error contraction}}\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}\xrightarrow[p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t}),t=T^{\prime}\to 1]{\text{reverse diffusion}}{\mathbf{x}_{0}}}_{\text{KDD-SemNOMA}},\vskip-3.41432pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">init</mtext></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝛀</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">KD-SemNOMA</mtext></munderover><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><msup><mi mathsize="0.710em">T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><msup><mi mathsize="0.710em">T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">init</mtext></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">error contraction</mtext></munderover><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">reverse diffusion</mtext></munderover><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>KDD-SemNOMA</mtext></munder><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{\mathbf{x}\xrightarrow[\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}=\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x};\mathbf{\Omega})]{\text{KD-SemNOMA}}\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}\xrightarrow[\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}\sim p(\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}\mid\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}})]{\text{error contraction}}\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}\xrightarrow[p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t}),t=T^{\prime}\to 1]{\text{reverse diffusion}}{\mathbf{x}_{0}}}_{\text{KDD-SemNOMA}},\vskip-3.41432pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\cdot;\mathbf{\Omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\cdot;\mathbf{\Omega})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the KD-SemNOMA model with trainable parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an intermediate diffused state, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> approximates <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Obtaining <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> via error contraction:</span> Defining the predicted error in KD-SemNOMA as <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}=\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}=\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> via the forward diffusion process</p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E24X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}+\sqrt{\left(1-\alpha_{T^{\prime}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜻</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}+\sqrt{\left(1-\alpha_{T^{\prime}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E24Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}\mathbf{x}-\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}\mathbf{e}+\sqrt{\left(1-\alpha_{T^{\prime}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜻</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}\mathbf{x}-\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}\mathbf{e}+\sqrt{\left(1-\alpha_{T^{\prime}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\zeta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\zeta}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\zeta}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{T^{\prime}}=\prod_{t=1}^{T^{\prime}}(1-\beta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{T^{\prime}}=\prod_{t=1}^{T^{\prime}}(1-\beta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the predefined noise variance at timestep <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The predicted error <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is contracted by a factor of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msqrt><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\sqrt{\alpha_{T^{\prime}}}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>), reducing the impact of artifacts introduced during KD-SemNOMA reconstruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sampling with diffusion priors:</span> Starting from <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ a reverse diffusion process using a pre-trained transition kernel</p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\sim p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t}),\quad t={T^{\prime}},{T^{\prime}-1},\dots,1,\vskip-4.2679pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\sim p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t}),\quad t={T^{\prime}},{T^{\prime}-1},\dots,1,\vskip-4.2679pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1};\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1};\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> from the DDIM (Eq. <a href="#S4.E21" title="In IV-A Preliminary ‣ IV Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). This iterative sampling refines <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, leveraging the diffusion model’s generative priors to enhance realism and detail of images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure fidelity across diverse multi-user images, we adopt the DDIM sampler with a randomness parameter <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. This approach mitigates excessive stochasticity, aligning <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> closely with <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving natural image characteristics. Additionally, we set the starting timestep <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=200</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling accelerated sampling compared to DDPM’s typical <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\approx 1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\approx 1000</annotation></semantics></math> steps, thus improving computational efficiency while maintaining high-quality refinement. In practice, we speed up the inference four times following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, and thus sample 50 steps for each testing image. The complete inference procedure is detailed in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-B Diffusion-Based Image Refinement ‣ IV Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The posterior <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{x}_{0}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{x}_{0}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}})</annotation></semantics></math> is implicitly constructed through the combination of the forward diffusion process <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{x}_{T^{\prime}}|\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}})</annotation></semantics></math> and the reverse sampling process <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}|\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}|\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Such an issue is more prominent under stringent bandwidth constraints or low SNR conditions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Inference procedure for diffusion model-based image refinement</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Test dataset <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{test}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{(N)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{test}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{(N)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, noise schedule <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, variance schedule <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Enhanced multi-user images <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(N)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(N)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference Phase (Enhancement with Pretrained Diffusion Model)</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            Input the initial reconstruction of KD-SemNOMA <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{(N)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{(N)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            Reshape <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times N\times C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times N\times C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{\text{flat}}\in\mathbb{R}^{(B\cdot N)\times C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mtext>flat</mtext></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}^{\text{flat}}\in\mathbb{R}^{(B\cdot N)\times C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            Sample intermediate state via diffusion process according to Eq. <a href="#S4.E24" title="In IV-B Diffusion-Based Image Refinement ‣ IV Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>:

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> down to <math id="alg2.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              Predict noise <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              Reverse sampling

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}}\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t-1}}\epsilon_{\theta}+\sigma_{t}\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}}\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t-1}}\epsilon_{\theta}+\sigma_{t}\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
            Reshape <math id="alg2.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}_{0}}^{\text{flat}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{B\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mtext>flat</mtext></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}_{0}}^{\text{flat}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{B\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}_{0}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times N\times C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}_{0}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times N\times C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(N)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\{\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(N)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Training and Evaluation</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Simulation Setup</span></h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span>1 </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For low-resolution datasets, we employ CIFAR-10 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> for training and testing. It consists of 50,000 training images and 10,000 testing images, each with the shape of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times W\times H=3\times 32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times W\times H=3\times 32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math>. For high-resolution datasets, we employ FFHQ dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> which comprises <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70,000</annotation></semantics></math> high-quality face images with a native resolution of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times W\times H=3\times 1024\times 1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1024</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times W\times H=3\times 1024\times 1024</annotation></semantics></math>, containing significant variations in age, race, and image background. For training, we down-sample the FFHQ images to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times W\times H=3\times 256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times W\times H=3\times 256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> using bi-cubic down-sampling.
In order to build dataset for multi-user scenarios, we randomly sample <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200,000</annotation></semantics></math> samples for the training set and dynamically group the samples from the original dataset into user specific batches. For the testing set, we divide the datasets into <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> non-overlapping groups and concatenate them into the multi-user dataset. Simultaneously, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly sampled from <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,20]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,20]</annotation></semantics></math> dB, and the Rayleigh parameters <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i},\phi_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i},\phi_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly generated to simulate diverse channel conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span>2 </span>Training and Inference Details</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train the KD-SemNOMA framework following Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Specifically, we employ the AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> with a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and a weight decay of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both the teacher and student models are trained using the batch size of 32. To mitigate overfitting, an early stopping mechanism is implemented, terminating training when no improvement in validation performance is observed over 10 consecutive epochs. During student model training, the loss weights are set to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}=100</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{3}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{3}=1</annotation></semantics></math> to balance the contributions of the restoration loss and distillation terms, as defined in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Knowledge Distillation Optimization for Semantic NOMA (KD-SemNOMA) ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>. During inference, KD-SemNOMA relies solely on the student model, incurring no additional computational overhead compared to the proposed SemNOMA model as detailed in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="II-C SemNOMA Network Architecture ‣ II System Model ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-C</span></a>. For DDIM inference, the first-stage output <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}=\hat{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{init}}=\hat{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is refined through iterative denoising (Eq. <a href="#S4.E23" title="In IV-B Diffusion-Based Image Refinement ‣ IV Diffusion Model-Based Image Refinement ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>), using <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=200</annotation></semantics></math> steps to maximize image perceptual performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For datasets of different resolutions, the encoder and decoder network parameters of the proposed ConvNeXt-based DeepJSCC are detailed in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A2 Training and Inference Details ‣ V-A Simulation Setup ‣ V Training and Evaluation ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> for CIFAR-10 and Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-A2 Training and Inference Details ‣ V-A Simulation Setup ‣ V Training and Evaluation ‣ Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> for FFHQ-256. The proposed framework and all comparative methods are implemented in PyTorch and executed on an NVIDIA 4090 GPU server.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Parameters setting of DeepJSCC encoder and decoder for the image resolution of 32×32</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Encoder Layer</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:90.4pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameters</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Input</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.5.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:90.4pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.5.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Input shape <math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S5.T1.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.5.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Stem (downsample)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.5.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:90.4pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.5.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Conv2D (<math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to 96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to 96</annotation></semantics></math>, kernel=4, stride=2)</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S5.T1.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.5.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Downsample Layer 1</span>
</span></td>
